From e0c5b3bc0ba80528e6251dbf3ffa8c2f2781d08b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paolo Severini <paolosev@microsoft.com>
Date: Tue, 7 Jul 2026 14:02:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [wasm interpreter] Sandbox hardening

Under the V8 sandbox threat model (attacker has arbitrary read/write
inside the sandbox cage), the DrumBrake interpreter trusted several
in-cage values that an attacker can corrupt, reaching trusted
(out-of-cage) state without a release-mode check. Harden those paths:

- Source the interpreter's module/native_module from
  WasmTrustedInstanceData instead of the in-cage
  WasmInstanceObject::module_object(), so a swapped module_object
  can no longer drive interpreter metadata.

- Bind the (in-cage) InterpreterHandle to its instance in
  WasmInterpreterRuntime::InstanceScope by requiring the published
  instance's trusted module to match the runtime's module. Checks every
  entry that accesses wasm_trusted_instance_data(), so a corrupted
  interpreter_object slot can no longer run another instance's handle.

- Validate func_ref in ExecuteCallRef with SBXCHECK(Is<WasmFuncRef>)
  before the unchecked cast, since the reference stack is
  attacker-corruptible.

- Add SBXCHECK bounds checks on memory indices and on trusted memory
  metadata (memory_base/memory_size, InitMemoryAddresses consistency)
  and on the call_indirect dispatch-table index, and upgrade
  InterpreterHandle::Execute signature/arity checks from DCHECK to
  SBXCHECK.

